  "plot_summary": "Spider-Man appears to rob a store, then escape from the pursuing police.&nbsp; Has he turned to crime?&nbsp; People debate the issue while reading the news reports, except that Flash Thompson doesn't appear convinced, and even Peter Parker himself begins to wonder if he's developed a split-personality.&nbsp; He's afraid to go to sleep even, wondering if this happens after he drops off at night.&nbsp; He needs to find out, so he visits a psychiatrist, but the thought of becoming too relaxed and revealing his identity makes him change his mind at the last minute.\n\nSoon, at the Bugle, Peter goes to see Jameson for a loan to help pay the mortgage - Aunt May's bank account is dangerously low - but Jameson refuses, telling him he only pays for photos.&nbsp; Peter decides to go out as Spider-Man and see if he can find some, but soon finds the public outraged at his appearance.&nbsp; It's worse than he imagined - the public hates him.\n\nNext day at school, Peter worries about money and whether he's committing crimes in his sleep when Liz Allan comes up to show him her new hairdo.&nbsp; He can't believe that she seems to be paying attention to him only because he's dating Betty, but Flash sees Liz as well (while defending Spider-Man to his friends) and puts his foot in his mouth, causing an icy response from Liz.&nbsp; (And I really like the word balloon with little icicles hanging off the bottom - very well done!)\n\nAt Jameson's office, the strange Mysterio arrives, promising to \"can get rid of Spider-Man single-handed.\"&nbsp; Before disappearing in a cloud of smoke, he demands that the Bugle print a notice for Spider-Man to meet him at the top of the Brooklyn Bridge.&nbsp; Sure enough, when Spider-Man arrives, Mysterio is there to greet him and immediately begins to demonstrate how his power is unique.&nbsp; He dissolves Spider-Man's webbing in mid-air and then disappears in a cloud of mist, punching Spider-Man inside it, whose spider sense seems to be jammed.&nbsp; Finally, Spider-Man sees he's getting nowhere and takes off, leaving Mysterio triumphant.\n\nNext day, a motorcade honors Mysterio, who defeated Spider-Man, but Flash Thompson watches, unconvinced, still telling everyone that Spider-Man is innocent.&nbsp; Even Peter seems surprised.\n\nSoon, Jameson is introducing Mysterio to the members of the Bugle staff.&nbsp; He's excited that Mysterio has agreed to give the Bugle an exclusive of him defeating and then unmasking Spider-Man in their next encounter, but when he shakes hands with Peter Parker (whom Jameson calls \"the best photographer I've got\"), Peter slips a spider-tracer into his cloak.\n\nSoon, Spider-Man tracks him down near a \"TV movie studio building.\"&nbsp; But Mysterio instantly summons the mist that dulls his spider sense, and seeing that Spider-Man is no threat to him, admits to impersonating him.&nbsp; He reveals that he was a movie stunt man that \"became a special effects man for TV movies.\"&nbsp; He could design and mimic most anything, so he decided to imitate Spider-Man and commit crimes as him and a separate identity as Mysterio for himself.&nbsp; He lets Spider-Man know how his suit works, with all the concealed devices inside to simulate magic.\n\nBut Spider-Man has been taping the entire monologue!&nbsp; Mysterio doesn't care because he's going to destroy Spider-Man.&nbsp; But this time, Spider-Man stays outside the cloud and punches into it until he finally connects, and the two spill into a nearby movie set and engage!&nbsp; Mysterio is indeed a skilled stunt man and is able to roll with many of the punches he takes, but Spider-Man finally destroys the jamming device that dulls his spider sense, and thus is able to connect with Mysterio even through the cloud he tries to hide in.&nbsp; (He remembers to set up his automatic camera to take photos, of course.)\n\nSoon, the defeated Mysterio sits at police headquarters, having been dropped there by Spider-Man, complete with the full confession.&nbsp; Jameson arrives to learn that Spider-Man is innocent after all and is dejected once again - he'll be a laughing stock - but his attitude changes when he returns to the Bugle to find the action photos Peter Parker has dropped off (his reaction on the last panel of page 21 is predictably hilarious - \"I'm saved!\" - heh).\n\nHe decides to be \"generous and pay him [Peter] almost <i>half</i> of what these pictures are worth!\"&nbsp; But Spider-Man arrives to tell him off, how he's always wrong about him - and naturally to web him to the ceiling to boot!\n\nBack at school, Flash Thompson is triumphant, having been proved right about Spider-Man, even while Peter Parker pretends not to trust the man \"any further than I can throw him!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "a solid enough introduction to one of Spider-Man's rogues gallery.&nbsp; A glory-seeking movie stunt man and special-effects guy turned to crime.&nbsp; It was a pretty insidious plan he came up with to first frame Spider-Man and then offer to nab him (for compensation of course).&nbsp; But naturally the monologue at the end did him in (as is always the case).\n\nI'll say it now for the record - I've never considered Mysterio all that interesting.&nbsp; I first encountered him in the pages of this mag as the book approached issue 200, and he didn't impress me then and still doesn't.&nbsp; But he is considered one of Spider-Man's main enemies, and I know he pops up here and there going forward and will try to treat him with the respect that a mainstay character demands.\n\nAs is often the case, the book got a significant boost from its supporting cast.&nbsp; Aunt May's money struggles, the hint that the Liz Allan/Betty Brant situation might get complicated, Jameson's continued efforts to discredit Spider-Man, and Flash Thompson showing strong support for his hero even while all others had lost faith.&nbsp; These little things added up to an extra boost in the bottom line - the book would otherwise have been right around mid-grade..&nbsp; But this mag continues to demonstrate how these side things are often just as important as the main plot-of-the-month, and it's a great aspect that seems missing in some of the other Marvel mags and makes the <i>Amazing Spider-Man </i>a solid read for the most part, issue after issue.\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\nSide Notes:\n\nThe top of page 10 has the phrase \"proportionate strength and agility of the invertebrate for whom he has named himself...\" - Spider-Man used the more common version of this phrase in issue #1 when he took on the Fantastic Four, but I don't recall having read this since (I might be wrong), so perhaps Stan finally decided, over a year later, that it was a good phrase to periodically throw into the text - just wanted to point that out.\n\nI laughed out loud when I read Marcelaine Wininger's letter that pleaded \"Don't let Aunt May die, please!&nbsp; It would ruin everything!\"&nbsp; (Nice to see such passion over a supporting cast member!)\n\nSteve Gillespie's letter also invoked a laugh when he stated:&nbsp; \"The one thing wrong with your comic is Ditko's art.&nbsp; He draws Spider-Man and the super villains fine, but the way he draws people makes them look like they have some rare disease!\" - to which the editor replies:&nbsp; <i>They have!&nbsp; It's called \"Ditko-itis.</i>  He's not wrong - Ditko <i>does</i> draw people kind of funny, and I've never been a big fan, but I've already gone on record to say that, for this magazine, and for these characters in particular, he really <i>has</i> nailed it.&nbsp; The quirky art here (and with Doctor Strange) just \"works\" - far more than it does anywhere else.&nbsp; Even David Catena agrees, in his letter from this issue, which includes \"I realize now that you and only you can draw Spider-Man the way he should be drawn.\"&nbsp; (And anyone who has seen the Kirby or Ayers renditions of him <i>must</i> be forced to agree - they really <i>are</i> sub-par.)",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}